In `length_of_largest_common_substring`, `max_length` is initialized to `0` and only changes when the condition `if dp[i][j] > max_length:` is true, at which point it is set to `dp[i][j]`. That means before each later evaluation, `max_length` stores the greatest `dp` value seen so far among earlier matching cells. But the current `dp[i][j]` is just the length of the common substring ending at the current `(i, j)` pair, computed as `dp[i - 1][j - 1] + 1` when the characters match. A later match can therefore produce a smaller value than an earlier maximum, so the relation `dp[i][j] >= max_length` is not guaranteed at every evaluation.

